::CW_U02_PRE_Q01::Passage: "I kept the ticket after the concert was canceled, pressing its bent corner flat every morning." What does the ticket most likely reveal?{
~A. The narrator dislikes music.#Incorrect. Keeping the ticket suggests attachment, not dislike.
=B. The narrator has an unresolved feeling connected to the canceled concert.#Correct. The repeated action shows the ticket carries emotional importance.
~C. The concert happened exactly as planned.#Incorrect. The passage says it was canceled.
~D. The ticket has no role in the scene.#Incorrect. The ticket is the central object.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q02::Passage: "You stand outside the office, rehearsing the truth until every version sounds like a lie." What point of view is used?{
~A. Third-person omniscient.#Incorrect. The passage does not use he, she, or they with access to many minds.
~B. First-person plural.#Incorrect. First-person plural uses "we."
~C. Objective camera-only narration.#Incorrect. The line includes inner rehearsal and judgment.
=D. Second person.#Correct. The narration addresses the character as "you."
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q03::Passage: Scene purpose: show that a character is hiding guilt. Which detail best supports the purpose?{
=A. The character wipes fingerprints from a glass after saying nothing happened.#Correct. The action contradicts the claim and suggests guilt.
~B. The character buys a sandwich.#Incorrect. This does not show guilt unless connected to the conflict.
~C. The character describes a sunny sky.#Incorrect. Weather alone does not show hidden guilt.
~D. The character reads a book title aloud.#Incorrect. This is not clearly connected to guilt.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q04::Passage: "The hallway seemed to narrow as I walked toward the audition room." What does the setting detail do?{
~A. It proves the building is changing shape literally.#Incorrect. The word "seemed" points to perception.
~B. It removes the audition conflict.#Incorrect. It intensifies the conflict.
=C. It mirrors the narrator's anxiety.#Correct. The narrowing hallway shows how pressure affects perception.
~D. It creates an unrelated science lesson.#Incorrect. The focus is emotional experience.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q05::Passage: A student begins a tense scene with four paragraphs of unrelated history. What is the best pacing advice?{
=A. Start closer to the tense moment and include only needed background.#Correct. This helps readers experience the scene's pressure directly.
~B. Keep delaying the tense moment.#Incorrect. Too much unrelated delay weakens tension.
~C. Add more unrelated history.#Incorrect. This increases the pacing problem.
~D. Remove the tense moment.#Incorrect. The tense moment is the scene's center.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q06::Passage: "The empty birdcage hung by the window, door still open." What can the object suggest?{
~A. A complete recipe.#Incorrect. No cooking details appear.
~B. A change to second person.#Incorrect. The object does not create point of view.
~C. No possible meaning.#Incorrect. The detail is emphasized and meaningful.
=D. Loss, escape, or absence in the scene.#Correct. The empty open cage is a focused detail readers can interpret.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q07::Passage: "Mae said she was ready, but she folded the permission slip smaller and smaller." What does the contrast show?{
~A. Mae has already submitted the slip.#Incorrect. She is still holding and folding it.
=B. Mae's actions complicate her spoken claim.#Correct. Her nervous action suggests she may not feel ready.
~C. Mae is not part of the scene.#Incorrect. Mae is the central character.
~D. Mae is giving a weather report.#Incorrect. No weather appears.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q08::Passage: Which statement best describes third-person limited narration?{
~A. It uses "I" and only the narrator's voice.#Incorrect. That is first person.
~B. It uses "you" to address the character.#Incorrect. That is second person.
=C. It uses third-person pronouns while staying close to one character's experience.#Correct. Third-person limited follows one character closely.
~D. It always reveals every character's thoughts.#Incorrect. That is closer to omniscient narration.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q09::Passage: "The trophy shelf was full, but Arin stared at the empty hook where the team jacket used to hang." What is emphasized?{
~A. Arin has never won anything.#Incorrect. The full trophy shelf suggests wins.
~B. The scene is about cleaning shelves.#Incorrect. The missing jacket creates emotional focus.
~C. The hook is unrelated.#Incorrect. It is the detail Arin stares at.
=D. The missing jacket matters more than the visible trophies in this moment.#Correct. The contrast directs attention to absence.
}

::CW_U02_PRE_Q10::Passage: A scene's purpose is to show friendship becoming strained. Which choice best fits?{
=A. Two friends avoid looking at the friendship bracelet on the desk.#Correct. The shared object and avoidance reveal strain.
~B. Two strangers discuss a recipe with no conflict.#Incorrect. This does not show friendship strain.
~C. A narrator lists every classroom rule.#Incorrect. Rules do not show the relationship problem.
~D. A character describes shoes unrelated to the friend.#Incorrect. The detail does not support the purpose.
}

